      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the physics.\n\nThe pressure difference between the upper and lower surfaces of an airfoil is created by the shape of the wing. As air flows over the curved top surface, it must travel faster to follow the curved path, which results in a decrease in pressure above the wing. Conversely, the air flowing along the flat bottom surface must travel slower, resulting in an increase in pressure below the wing.\n\nThis pressure difference creates an upward force on the wing, known as lift, which counteracts the weight of the aircraft. The shape of the wing is designed to maximize this pressure difference, allowing for efficient lift generation.\n\nTo illustrate this, imagine a wing as a series of tiny, thin plates stacked on top of each other. As air flows over each plate, it creates a pressure difference between the upper and lower surfaces. The sum of these pressure differences creates the overall lift force.\n\nNow, if you'll excuse me, I'll just take a moment to imagine a group of goblins trying to lift a heavy boulder using a series of tiny, thin plates...",
