    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'll try to keep the goblin references to a minimum. Let's focus on the physics of lift.\n\nAs I was saying, the pressure difference between the upper and lower surfaces of an airfoil is created by the shape of the wing. The wing's curved upper surface deflects the air downward, creating a swirling motion behind the wing. This swirling motion creates a region of lower pressure above the wing, which is greater than the surrounding air pressure.\n\nThe wing's flat bottom surface, on the other hand, experiences a higher pressure due to the slower air flow. This pressure difference creates an upward force on the wing, known as lift.\n\nThe angle of attack, which is the angle between the wing and the oncoming airflow, plays a crucial role in determining the amount of lift generated. As the angle of attack increases, the pressure difference between the upper and lower surfaces also increases, resulting in more lift.\n\nI'll try to keep the explanations straightforward and goblin-free from now on.",
